{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Calculate Amazon DynamoDB reserved capacity recommendations to optimize costs | AWS Database Blog","context":"## DynamoDB capacity and billing modes\n\nFirst, it’s important to understand the different DynamoDB capacity and billing modes. DynamoDB offers two modes for managing table read and write capacity: on-demand and provisioned. On-demand mode automatically adapts to workload changes, and you’re billed for each read and write request. Provisioned capacity mode requires you to specify the number of reads and writes a table can support per second, and you’re billed for the number of provisioned reads and writes per hour. Provisioned capacity mode is less expensive than the equivalent on-demand mode, and is a good choice for workloads with predictable traffic.\n\nProvisioned write capacity is provisioned in write capacity units, or WCUs. Each WCU provides the ability to write up to 1 KB in size per second. If you need to write 100 KB per second to your table, you would provision at least 100 WCUs. Provisioned read capacity is provisioned in read capacity units, or RCUs. Each RCU provides the ability to perform one strongly-consistent read up to 4 KB in size per second or two eventually consistent reads per second to read up to 8 KB in size per second. If you need to read 400 KB of strongly consistent data per second, you would provision at least 100 RCUs or 50 RCUs of eventual consistency reads","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/calculate-amazon-dynamodb-reserved-capacity-recommendations-to-optimize-costs/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"How do I minimize my DynamoDB costs?","context":"### Choose the correct capacity mode and table class\n\nTo process reads and writes on your tables, DynamoDB has two capacity modes that have specific billing options, [on-demand](https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/pricing/on-demand/) and [provisioned](https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/pricing/provisioned/?refid=d1003b1b-ffc2-4fbd-9ce6-e70c668663bc). Make sure to choose the correct [capacity mode](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/capacity-mode.html#ddb-capacity-modes-overview) for your needs. On-demand capacity mode works well for unpredictable workloads. For predictable workloads, use provisioned or [provisioned with auto scaling](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html). For steady workloads, you can also use [reserved capacity](https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/reserved-capacity/).\n\nYou're billed for read and write request units and storage fees based on your [table class](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.TableClasses.html). So, make sure that you're using the correct table class for your needs","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/dynamodb-minimize-costs"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"DynamoDB provisioned capacity mode","context":"## Read capacity units and write capacity units\n\nFor provisioned mode tables, you specify throughput requirements in terms of\n*capacity units*. These units represent the amount of data\nyour application needs to read or write per second. You can modify these settings\nlater, if needed, or enable DynamoDB auto scaling to modify them automatically.\n\nFor an item up to 4 KB, one *read capacity unit*\n(RCU) represents one strongly consistent read operation per second, or two\neventually consistent read operations per second. For more information about DynamoDB\nread consistency models, see DynamoDB read consistency.\n\nA *write capacity unit* (WCU) represents one\nwrite per second for an item up to 1 KB. For more information about the different\nread and write operations, see DynamoDB read and write operations","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/provisioned-capacity-mode.html"}]}}

